{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0470-linear-regression-under-model-uncertainty.md", "equation": "Model: Y = βᵀX + η + ε (1.1), where η is a nonlinear random variable capturing mean uncertainty (values in [μ̱,μ̄]) and ε follows the G-normal distribution N_G(0,[σ̱²,σ̄²]) capturing variance uncertainty; together they encompass an infinite family of classical distributions via the representation E[φ(ε)] = max_{θ∈Θ} E_θ[φ(ε)] (Θ = L² processes into [σ̱,σ̄]). Two population optima under convex loss φ: upper β̄*(φ) = argmin_β E[φ(Y − βᵀX − η)] (1.2, min-max) and lower β̱*(φ) = argmin_β −E[−φ(Y − βᵀX − η)] (1.3, min-min). Lemma 2.1: min-max = max-min and min-min = min-min (eqs. 2.3–2.4). Theorem 2.1 (main result): for convex φ, β̄*(φ) equals the classical optimum under N(μ_{θ̄_φ}, σ̄²) and β̱*(φ) under N(μ_{θ̱_φ}, σ̱²) — i.e., infinite-family uncertainty collapses to two classical normals. For square loss: U-LSE β̄* is the OLS optimum on data Y = βᵀX + μ_{σ̄} + ε′, ε′ ∼ N(0,σ̄²) (3.2); L-LS ...", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9126}
