{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0477-econometrics-for-decision-making-building-foundations.md", "equation": "State space S, choice set C, welfare w(c,s); SDF c(ψ) over sample space Ψ; sampling distributions (Q_s, s∈S). Decision criteria without data: (1) max_{c∈C} ∫w(c,s)dπ (subjective average welfare); (2) max_{c∈C} min_{s∈S} w(c,s) (maximin); (3) min_{c∈C} max_{s∈S}[max_{d∈C} w(d,s)−w(c,s)] (MMR). With data: (4) max ∫E_s{w[c(ψ),s]}dπ; (5) maximin over E_s{w}; (6) min max over s of oracle gap (MMR). Binary choice: regret = R_{c(∙)s}· | , eqs. (7)–(8). Model space S_m replaces S; as-if optimization: c[s(ψ)] ∈ argmax_{c∈C} w[c,s(ψ)], eq. (9). Set-estimate as-if problems (1′)–(3′) act as if state space is S(ψ). Hodges–Lehmann (1950) MMR predictor for [0,1] outcomes: (μ_N√N + ½)/(√N + 1). Missing-data midpoint predictor max regret: ¼[P(δ=1)²/K + P(δ=0)²]. MMR fractional allocation (eq. 10): z_MMR = E[y(b) | δ(b)=1]·p + {1−E[y(a) | δ(a)=1]}(1−p); max regret z_MMR(1−z_MMR) (fractional) vs min(z_MMR, ...", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9126}
{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0477-econometrics-for-decision-making-building-foundations.md", "equation": "Prediction: midpoint predictor always beats the sample-average-of-observed-outcomes predictor when all distributions are feasible — max MSE ≈¼ the size when observability ≤0.8, ≈½ at observability 0.9 (Tables 1A vs 2A); identification (bias from missing data) dominates imprecision when observability <0.7 (max MSE ≈ ¼P(δ=0)²); the Panel-B constraint on distribution differences does not change Table 1 entries (max-MSE states satisfy the constraint). Treatment: Utah example — max regret of mandate-confinement (a) = 0.45 vs mandate-no-confinement (b) = 0.55, so (a) minimizes maximum regret, yet the ES rule picks (b) because 0.41>0.23 — as-if optimization selects the MMR-inferior treatment. AMMR rule: max regret invariant in p, rises from ≈0.34 (N=25) to ≈0.40 (N=100) — more data makes the deterministic rule *less* randomized and *more* regrettable, approaching ½ (footnotes: N=1 gives max reg ...", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9126}
{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0478-statistical-inference-for-statistical-decisions.md", "equation": "Criteria (1)–(3) without data (maximize subjective average welfare; maximin; MMR), statistical versions (4)–(6) over feasible SDFs Γ. Binary choice: regret = R_{c(ψ)s}· | , eqs. (7)–(8); Neyman-Pearson convention: Q_s[c(ψ)=b]≤α for s∈S_a, α=0.05 typical. As-if optimization: c[s(ψ)]∈argmax w[c,s(ψ)] (9). ES-rule max-regret bounds (Manski & Tetenov 2016, Props. 1–2), K arms, outcome range M, n/arm balanced: (10) (2e)^{−½}M(K−1)n^{−½}; (11) M(ln K)^{½}n^{−½}. MMR fractional allocation with missing data (Manski 2007b, eq. 12): z*(b)=1 if (e_a−y_{1a})p_a+(y_{0b}−e_b)p_b+(y_{1a}−y_{0b})<0; =0 if symmetric condition; else [e_bp_b+y_1(1−p_b)−e_ap_a−y_0(1−p_a)]/[(y_1−y_0)(2−p)] (eq. 13); sample analog (14)/(16) is finite-sample MMR when p is known (Prop. 2, uses E[z_N(b)]=z*(b)). Assumptions: outcomes bounded [y_{0t},y_{1t}]; individualistic treatment response; random selection may be a model rat ...", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9126}
{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0478-statistical-inference-for-statistical-decisions.md", "equation": "Cancer example: conventional test (α=0.05, β=0.20) → Type I regret 1/30 yr, Type II regret 4/5 yr, max regret 4/5 yr; reversed test (α=0.20, β=0.05) → regrets 2/15 and 1/5 yr, max regret 1/5 yr — the unconventional test has 4× smaller max regret. Manski & Tetenov (2016), N=145/arm: ES rule max regret 0.01 vs one-sided z-test 0.05; ES regret peaks at effect size ≈±0.03 (error prob ≈0.35); z-test regret peaks at effect size ≈0.08 (error prob ≈0.6). ES-rule bounds: (10) tighter for K=2,3 treatments; (11) tighter for K≥4. Midpoint predictor max regret ¼[P(δ=1)²/N + P(δ=0)²]. Curious finite-sample result (Prop. 2): under its assumptions there is no MMR advantage to large sample size — one observation per treatment is as good as the full sample. All numbers are the paper's claims.", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9126}
