{"file": "C:/Users/Garrett/Sports/docs/arxiv-program/research/2026-09-21/arxiv-deep/0492-analysing-skill-predominance-in-generalized-fantasy.md", "equation": "(a) Score model: (P_1,…,P_n)∼LogNormal(μ,Σ), i.e. (log P_1,…,log P_n)∼N(μ,Σ), equicorrelated; win probabilities π_i=P(P_i≥P_j ∀j≠i) estimated by Monte Carlo. (b) Player behavior: analytical players sample beliefs p∼Dirichlet(απ) with concentration α calibrated so p is within β of π with probability ≥1−δ, via Chebyshev + union bound: α≥n/(4δβ²)−1 (Appendix A); then team choice ∼Multinomial(1,p). Random players choose ∼Multinomial(1,1_n/n). (c) Payoff: shared prize pool — if team i wins, its N_i selectors split the pool (e.g., 20,000/N_i). (d) Metrics: Selection Ratio = (analytical share on team i)/(random share on team i); Mean Winnings including zeros. (e) Extensions: number of experts ∈{2,3,4}; correlation ρ∈{0.1,…,0.9}; Impact Player: 4 candidate impact players (I_1,…,I_4)∼LogNormal(μ_I,Σ_I), ρ_I=0.3, independent of P; lowest-contributor share S_i∼DirichletMin(α) (minimum coordinate of ...", "page_or_eqnum": null, "wave": 9126}
